chicago fire chicago med chicago pd and  for a while chicago justice  what's the story of all these shows why  are they all set in chicago  keep watching for the behind the scenes  details  over a decades-long career dick wolfe  had brought many shows to television  including hits like the law order  franchise in the early 2010s wolf  decided to shift his focus from cops to  big city firefighters  wolff contacted nbc and they soon  brought screenwriters michael brandt and  derek hoss on board to develop a pilot  though they had a number of big screen  hits to their credit including too fast  too furious and 310 to yuma they had  little experience in television but bran  told the hollywood reporter that lack of  experience is part of why they got hired  dick was looking to do something totally  different and they were looking for  feature writers wolff and nbc liked the  pilot and by 2012 chicago fire was in  production with a slot on nbc's  primetime schedule  the reason the first of many one chicago  shows was set in chicago is because it  was the perfect locale  according to brent a show about first  responders was too touchy for new york  because quote september 11th is still a  big subject there while los angeles  doesn't have enough weather to be  dramatic  chicago fire chicago pd and chicago med  are all connected sharing the same  fictional reality under the one chicago  umbrella  but characters from every show  frequently pop up in other seemingly  unrelated series as well as mastermind  dick wolf has created many shows that  all apparently take place in the same  universe  for example 2014 chicago fire and  chicago pd crossed past with law and  order svu it proved so successful that a  few months later all those shows crossed  paths again telling a story that was  initially supposed to be a two-part svu  episode and in 2017 one arson story  dominated episodes of chicago fire  chicago pd chicago med and the  short-lived chicago justice but the  legal element presided over by judge  daniel melnick a character from the  original law and order which had been  cancelled in 2010 before the one chicago  universe even existed the crossovers can  even transcend the gulf between networks  in 2020 cbs's fbi a dick wolf production  featured chicago pd character haley  upton teaming up with the fbi's agent  o.a in new york  actor anthony ferraris has played rescue  squad 3 truck driver tony on all three  major 1 chicago shows with over 100  episodes of chicago fire and cameos on  both chicago pd and chicago med  not only does his character share his  name he shares his profession too  ferraris is a native chicagoan and an  active firefighter in fact he wasn't  even a professional actor before he was  cast on chicago fire in 2012  so how did he land the job  well a friend was told by a  firefighter's union that the show was  looking for extras and he asked ferraris  to accompany him on the audition  after he got the gig his involvement  increased on the first day of shooting  the crew discovered that none of the  actors cast as firefighters could drive  the emergency vehicles ferrara said the  show's technical advisor who i've known  for years asked me if i would drive i  said sure and that's pretty much how it  all went down after a half dozen  episodes co-creator derek hoss told  ferraris he was going to start writing  lines for him and that technical advisor  who got ferraris into the spotlight that  was steve chicarotis a chicago  firefighter who's also appeared in more  than 20 episodes of chicago fire along  with helping to write and serving as an  associate producer sophia bush was one  of the main stars of chicago pd at its  beginning in 2014  the most recognizable member of the cast  thanks to her time on the hit teen soap  one tree hill bush portrayed the  complicated aaron lindsay a former  juvenile offender who becomes a police  informant before working her way up to  chicago police detective  at the end of the fourth season of  chicago pd though detective lindsay  abruptly left her hard-won position  accepting a spot with the fbi's  counterterrorism squad in new york city  so as to shield her mother the subject  of a murder investigation in reality  bush's character was written out because  the actor had decided to leave chicago  pd  the reason as she told dax shepard's  armchair expert pondcast bush found the  show's working conditions to be  deplorable  it was a consistent onslaught barrage of  abusive behavior bush said that her body  began breaking down because of the  constant stress  at first producers wouldn't let bush out  of her contract so she played hardball  threatening to sue to get out of it or  go public with her criticisms in a new  york times op-ed  and that seemed to do the trick  oliver flatt is among the most acclaimed  american actors of his generation lauded  for performances on the small screen big  screen and broadway he's been nominated  for four emmy awards he received various  accolades for his appearances in films  like frost nixon and please gif and he  was a tony award nominee in 2006 for the  play shining city and yet the thing for  which he's arguably best known is  chicago met so how did platt wind up  doing network tv  it's all owing to his connection with  one chicago architect dick wolf  solidified over another tv show that  didn't succeed platt told the hollywood  reporter dick and i did another show  together way back in 2000 dick and i  formed a very real friendship and i have  a tremendous respect for dick as a  producer  platt said he was particularly  interested to explore mental health in  the er there's no no x-ray for  anxiety right and sort of adding that  element into this environment was to be  an interesting interesting character to  explore while all the chicago shows can  stand on their own nbc lumps the  connected series together and airs them  in succession on chicago wednesdays  that's one full night of shows and it  would seem about as much chicago drama  that the marketplace can hold or  audiences can tolerate  on march 1 2017 nbc debuted chicago  justice the fourth entry in producer  dick wolf's one chicago saga about windy  city public servants and first  responders the courtroom drama bore some  similarities to the order portion of  wolf's long-running law and order as  well as some connections  lead character and prosecutor peter  stone was the son of law order attorney  ben stone  chicago justice settled into its run as  a mid-season replacement after  premiering as the tail end of a  three-show crossover with chicago fire  and chicago pd despite its link to those  hit series chicago justice was one  chicago series too many after just 13  episodes were met with much lower  ratings than those of its sister series  nbc sent chicago justice away  once one chicago producers decide to  make a spin-off they generally do so  efficiently but chicago med's rollout  was complicated and rushed the april  2015 chicago fire installment i am the  apocalypse introduced chicago med's  premise and the standalone show  premiered just seven months later it was  actually set to debut in early 2016 but  then nbc fast tracked it for a fall  debut after the medical drama  heartbreaker was suddenly postponed due  to the pregnancy of star melissa george  then in august med star lori holden  abruptly quit the show citing family  reasons days later and mere weeks before  filming was set to begin showrunner  andrew detman left over creative clashes  with production company universal  television given all these hurdles it's  a miracle the show became the hit it is  one day in december 2012  local chicago program wgn morning news  carried a harrowing and startling story  about a plane crash within city limits  with overhead footage obtained by its  helicopter team wgn aired video of the  wreckage depicting a small downed plane  on the corner of chicago's 29th street  and martin luther king drive also  reporting that roads around the site of  the accident had been closed to traffic  a tragedy for sure but it turned out to  have a happy ending for everyone except  journalists a few moments after  delivering the news of the plane crash  anchors larry potash and robin  baumgarten issued an update the plane  crash wasn't a real crash at all merely  a simulated one as it turns out it was a  setup for a scene in chicago fire which  had only just recently begun shooting  its first season in the windy city  and irate baumgarden said on the air  they might want to tell the news folks  when they're doing this and shutting  down king drive are you kidding me  potash then helpfully added just to be  clear the other stories in that news  block were real  just the last one not so much people  contain multitudes and that certainly  held true for chicago pd's sean roman  at the end of chicago pd's third season  it looked like the hot-headed roman was  a goner after he was shot in the line of  duty however he somehow survived only to  disappear from the show anyway after  choosing to quit the force and move away  to san diego rather than be relegated to  semi-active police duty so why did the  sudden departure happen  actually it was more or less planned  right from the beginning of the series  series creator dick wolf really wanted  actor brian garrity on the show but  garrity was unsure about signing the  seven year contract that is typical for  stars of shows like chicago pd so to  accommodate garrity wolfe instead agreed  to allow garrity to sign a year-to-year  deal so he could quote leave the show  when he felt it was time to move on  nbc gives over a lot of its prime time  schedule each week of the regular season  to dick wolf's one chicago franchise  with an entire night of programming  that's a substantial commitment to a  franchise that hasn't always proven to  be a popular or profitable endeavor  despite how procedural shows are usually  a safe bet for networks witness the  success of ncis and law and order shows  none of the three exton chicago series  were hits right out of the gate even the  ones that were launched on the backs of  previous chicago shows that had evolved  into hits chicago fire debuted in fall  2012 and it finished its first season as  only the 51st most watched show on  network tv nbc renewed it and used it to  launch the police-centered spin-off  chicago pd in january 2014 which  finished its first season at number 61.  chicago med did a little better in its  inaugural season finishing at number 37  in the 2015-16 television season but nbc  stuck with the shows letting the cast  and creative staff find their voice and  allowing time for an audience to build  it worked in the  2019-2020 season all three parts of one  chicago ranked near the top of the  nielsen tv ratings chicago fire came in  at number seven followed by chicago pd  and chicago med tied at number 10. in  terms of total average viewers one  chicago draws more people than cbs's  multi-programmed ncis juggernaut the  2020 deaths of george floyd and brianna  taylor at the hands of police officers  in minnesota and kentucky set off an  international wave of civil rights  protests not seen in the u.s since the  1960s while americans publicly and  privately grappled with questions of  systemic racism law enforcement  overreach and police brutality it left  the entertainment industry in a strange  place  long the hallmark of television programs  that celebrated or glorified police  suddenly felt dated at best and  totalitarian at worst law enforcement  reality shows live pv and cops were  quickly cancelled and several cast  members both past and present of nbc's  law enforcement heavy one chicago  franchise publicly supported police  reform in the black lives matter  movement those actors may have  ultimately forced the hand of their  bosses to address the issues on their  purportedly gritty realistic and ripped  from the headlines tv series also  spurring action civil rights advocacy  group color of change co-authored a 2020  study called normalizing injustice which  argue that tv shows chicago pd in  particular normalize police misbehavior  so in september 2020 nicole ari parker  joined the cast of chicago pd as deputy  superintendent samantha miller a police  reform advocate who comes to chicago to  assist voight and his team follows  strict new police protocols the days of  playing god are over  the quicker you understand that the  better  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite tv personalities are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]  you
 

